# Every text file is stored with LF line endings, whatever the editor, the
# shell or the coding agent wrote: ``git add`` converts CRLF to LF. The rule
# wins over each machine's ``core.autocrlf``, so a Windows checkout no longer
# commits whole-file CRLF rewrites that show every line as changed and break
# blame - and shell scripts never reach a container with CRLF, where POSIX
# ``/bin/sh`` (dash) reads the trailing ``\r`` as part of an option name and
# refuses to start with ``Illegal option -``.
# Pinned by backend/tests/test_repo_text_hygiene.py.
* text=auto eol=lf

# A fixture whose bytes are the test (a CRLF payload, a bare \r) is marked
# here with ``-text``: Git stores it byte for byte and the hygiene test skips
# it. Example: backend/tests/data/crlf_payload.eml -text

# Windows-native scripts (PowerShell, batch, NSIS) are checked out with CRLF
# (and stored as LF). They stay ASCII: Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads BOM-less
# UTF-8 as cp1252.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.nsh text eol=crlf
*.nsi text eol=crlf

# Binaries are never converted (text=auto detects them; these are explicit).
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.mp3 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.wav binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
